{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"blind_id\":\"HOX033\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"Says recoupment is usually not a separate claim, but does not state that waived amounts in this arrangement are permanently forgone and allows for contractual recoupment.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"States that ordinary expense-limitation amounts are typically recoupable for up to three years, subject to the lower of the cap at waiver and recovery.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":\"“Where a contractual waiver includes recoupment, it is typically limited to a short look-back” — this conflicts with the supplied bank unitary-fee arrangement, where waived amounts are permanently forgone.\",\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The ordinary expense-cap rule is covered, but the unitary-fee treatment is incomplete and includes a material conflicting claim. The answer does not reverse the arrangements or claim unlimited recovery.\"}"}}
